00:06:32.590 --> 00:06:34.180
What's container security?

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:36.270
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Yeah,
that's a great question.

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:38.020
Let's start with like,
do we assume people

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:39.040
know what a container is?

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:39.445
Is that a fair assumption?

00:06:39.445 --> 00:06:40.940
MARK MANDEL: Let's go there.

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:42.010
Yeah, high level.

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:42.450
MELANIE WARRICK: Great question.

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:44.365
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, why
don't we start there?

00:06:44.365 --> 00:06:45.240
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: OK.

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:47.199
So a container is a
simpler way of packaging

00:06:47.199 --> 00:06:49.240
some of your software up
to make it more portable

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:51.760
and do better bin
packing on your computer.

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:54.529
So rather than having a VM,
which is somebody else's

00:06:54.529 --> 00:06:56.320
or your hardware, and
you just kind of care

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:58.990
about the OS, a container you
care about even less than that.

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:01.540
You just package the application
with binaries and libraries

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:03.190
into a container.

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:04.937
And then you can run
it on a minimal OS

00:07:04.937 --> 00:07:07.270
that can be kind of arbitrary
and anywhere environments.

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:09.430
The idea being you can move
this thing around really easily,

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:11.775
but it's kind of hard to
manage these things at scale,

00:07:11.775 --> 00:07:13.900
which is what Google learned
more than a decade ago

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:14.830
internally.

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:17.620
And so internally, we created
Borg a long, long time ago

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:19.870
and externally created
Kubernetes about four years ago

00:07:19.870 --> 00:07:20.110
now.

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:22.120
And Kubernetes is a way for
you to-- it's a container

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:23.106
orchestration platform.

00:07:23.106 --> 00:07:24.730
It's a way for you
to manage containers

00:07:24.730 --> 00:07:27.640
in terms of right-sizing them,
scaling things up and down,

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:30.890
load balancing, et cetera,
groups of containers.

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:32.005
So container security--

00:07:32.005 --> 00:07:32.880
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes.

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:33.380
Yes, that.

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:35.090
MARK MANDEL: I will
make a small segue.

00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:37.430
Episode 46, we did an episode
on Borg with John Wilkes.

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:38.750
So if you want to
learn more about Borg,

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:40.990
you can go back and hear about
the history of Kubernetes.

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:41.450
Anyway, please continue.

00:07:41.450 --> 00:07:42.582
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Awesome.

00:07:42.582 --> 00:07:44.830
So we're thinking about
container security at Google

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:47.376
in three main pillars,
or three main areas.

00:07:47.376 --> 00:07:49.750
The first one is what we'll
call infrastructure security.

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:51.208
So making sure that
your containers

00:07:51.208 --> 00:07:52.390
are secure to develop.

00:07:52.390 --> 00:07:55.420
So this is around configurations
you're putting into Kubernetes

00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:56.650
or Kubernetes Engine--

00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:59.544
things like seeker management,
networking, identity and access

00:07:59.544 --> 00:08:00.460
management, et cetera.

00:08:00.460 --> 00:08:02.180
All these things that you need
to have in your infrastructure

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:03.552
for this thing to work.

00:08:03.552 --> 00:08:06.010
The second area is what we're
calling software supply chain

00:08:06.010 --> 00:08:08.570
or making sure that your
containers are secure for--

00:08:08.570 --> 00:08:11.062
your container images are
secure to build and deploy.

00:08:11.062 --> 00:08:12.520
So how do you know
that your images

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:13.630
are free of vulnerabilities?

00:08:13.630 --> 00:08:15.670
How do you know what the
dependencies that you have are,

00:08:15.670 --> 00:08:17.410
that it came out of your
build pipeline, et cetera,

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:18.700
before you deploy them?

00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:21.325
And then the third area is what
we're calling container runtime

00:08:21.325 --> 00:08:24.250
security, or making sure
that your containers are

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:26.267
secure at runtime.

00:08:26.267 --> 00:08:27.600
And this is around making sure--

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:29.240
it's traditional
security operations.

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:31.150
So if somebody attacks
your container,

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:33.400
if you have an attempted
container escape, et cetera,

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:35.982
how do you identify
that, mitigate it?

00:08:35.982 --> 00:08:37.690
Can you could do
forensics after the fact

00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:39.731
to see what actually
happened in your environment

00:08:39.731 --> 00:08:40.780
and then track that?

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:42.946
MELANIE WARRICK: So what
is different about securing

00:08:42.946 --> 00:08:44.592
containers compared to VMs?

00:08:44.592 --> 00:08:45.550
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Sure.

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:47.980
I mean, at a high level,
not a lot in the sense

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:50.740
that I think security people are
very worried about this model.

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:52.750
We're like, ah, I don't know
what these containers are.

00:08:52.750 --> 00:08:53.770
I don't know what Kubernetes is.

00:08:53.770 --> 00:08:55.840
I don't want to have to
learn all this new stuff.

00:08:55.840 --> 00:08:56.590
It's not that bad.

00:08:56.590 --> 00:08:58.240
If you had a network
firewall before,

00:08:58.240 --> 00:08:59.380
you still need a
network firewall.

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:00.910
If you had an IDS IPS
before, you still need one.

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:02.320
You still need
identity management.

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:03.280
You still need
seeker management.

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:04.530
All these things are the same.

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:06.500
The plugability points
a little bit different.

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:08.041
I think some of the
main differences,

00:09:08.041 --> 00:09:10.660
though, are one is this minimal
OS, as I mentioned earlier.

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:14.380
Compared to a VM, you can have
a much smaller host operating

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:16.600
system than you do
on a virtual machine.

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:18.600
And what that means is
if you're doing it right,

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:21.370
that thing is tiny and you
patch that super regularly.

00:09:21.370 --> 00:09:23.080
It shouldn't have
any vulnerabilities

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:24.310
that somebody can exploit.

00:09:24.310 --> 00:09:26.230
And that actually makes
your overall system safer,

00:09:26.230 --> 00:09:28.021
because you have smaller
surface of attack.

00:09:28.021 --> 00:09:31.750
Another major difference between
containers and VMs is, I think,

00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:33.820
one of the talking points
against containers,

00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:35.785
as people were saying, hey, I
don't have as good isolation

00:09:35.785 --> 00:09:36.700
with containers.

00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:38.158
I don't have this
hypervisor, which

00:09:38.158 --> 00:09:40.640
I have with VMs, that provides
strong isolation, security

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:42.640
isolation between my systems.

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:45.255
And that was sort of true
until Google released

00:09:45.255 --> 00:09:47.380
gVisor and these other
projects in the open source,

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:49.540
like Kata containers and
Nabla containers that

00:09:49.540 --> 00:09:51.890
provide container-level
or actually pod-level,

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:53.470
in Kubernetes, isolation.

00:09:53.470 --> 00:09:56.344
So one of the counterarguments
against using containers

00:09:56.344 --> 00:09:58.760
from a security point of view
is now one of its strengths.

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:02.222
You can use the same strong
isolation at a container level

00:10:02.222 --> 00:10:04.180
and still have the bin
packing and the benefits

00:10:04.180 --> 00:10:06.220
of using containers
that you were

00:10:06.220 --> 00:10:07.600
aiming for in the first place.

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:08.500
MARK MANDEL: Does
that mean that we

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:10.060
should be running all our
containers under something

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:10.840
like gVisor?

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:12.299
Or is it fine what
we're doing now?

00:10:12.299 --> 00:10:14.048
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
That's a great question.

00:10:14.048 --> 00:10:16.450
I think gVisor is meant to be
a most-purpose sandbox, not

00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:17.880
an all-purpose sandbox.

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:20.550
So gVisor filters
about 150 comments as

00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:23.833
calls, and prevents a
process from running things

00:10:23.833 --> 00:10:24.840
that it shouldn't run.

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:26.760
It's not going to be perfect for
every single application you're

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:28.600
going to want to run
in your environment.

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:30.725
That being said, we use
gVisor internally at Google

00:10:30.725 --> 00:10:32.310
to run some of our services.

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:34.800
And I'll also kind of answer
not a question that you asked.

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:36.690
You said, should I run all
my containers in gVisor?

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:38.981
I'll say, should you run all
your things on containers?

00:10:38.981 --> 00:10:41.760
What a great question, Mark!

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:44.670
Not necessarily, but that's
certainly Google's model.

00:10:44.670 --> 00:10:47.070
Some of the reasons we can
do things like live migration

00:10:47.070 --> 00:10:48.460
is because we have Borg.

00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:50.680
So we have these whole
packages of things,

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:53.520
including your containers which
run inside of our containers,

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:55.230
and we can move them around
easily and patch the underlying

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:56.840
infrastructure without
taking you down.

00:10:56.840 --> 00:10:59.010
And that's the beauty of using
something like containers.

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:00.760
MELANIE WARRICK:
Actually, on a side note,

00:11:00.760 --> 00:11:02.880
I'm curious, what would
you not run on containers?

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:03.330
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
I think anything

00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:06.360
that has specific hardware
dependencies I would probably

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:09.130
not attempt to do
right now at least.

00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:11.820
So if you have some very, very
custom stuff on bare metal

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:15.847
on-prem, like it's OK, like not
the first workload I'd go for.

00:11:15.847 --> 00:11:17.430
And then the other
end of the spectrum

00:11:17.430 --> 00:11:18.540
is the functions, right?

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:20.880
Something that's super simple
that it's too heavyweight

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:22.000
to spin up a container.

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:22.542
Don't bother.

00:11:22.542 --> 00:11:24.625
MARK MANDEL: So actually,
I was going to follow up

00:11:24.625 --> 00:11:26.836
and say, is it also possibly
a question of what trust

00:11:26.836 --> 00:11:28.710
you have in the software
that you're running?

00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:29.960
If you're like,
this is my software.

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:31.020
I know where it came from.

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:31.800
And I know what it's doing.

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:32.770
So I'm like, that's OK.

00:11:32.770 --> 00:11:35.280
But if I don't know where
the software is coming from,

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:37.170
then maybe that's something I'd
put inside gVisor or something

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:37.610
like that.

00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:38.430
Would that be fair?

00:11:38.430 --> 00:11:40.054
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
That's a great point.

00:11:40.054 --> 00:11:42.450
So we think about software
and the level of trust

00:11:42.450 --> 00:11:43.580
that it has as a continuum.

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:44.550
Nothing is fully trusted.

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:45.690
Nothing is fully untrusted.

00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:48.990
This is very security
person messaging.

00:11:48.990 --> 00:11:52.055
But more trusted software might
be software that you build,

00:11:52.055 --> 00:11:54.180
might be software that your
developers have signed,

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:56.040
et cetera, that's
from you internally.

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:58.411
And yeah, you're probably
OK running one developer's

00:11:58.411 --> 00:12:00.660
software from your company
next to another developer's

00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:01.160
software.

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:03.660
And that's fine, short
of some insider risk.

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:05.160
On the other on the
spectrum, you're

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:07.620
running some third-party
arbitrary code,

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:10.080
some plug-in that you downloaded
from GitHub that you don't

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:11.700
really know what's in there.

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:14.610
We have some customers who
are running their customers

00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:16.450
provided data in containers.

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:18.450
And in that particular
case, I would be worried.

00:12:18.450 --> 00:12:20.190
Not so much about
a container escape,

00:12:20.190 --> 00:12:23.340
but like around one workload
affecting another workload

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:24.560
in terms of resource use.

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:26.310
MARK MANDEL: So
actually-- and I think you

00:12:26.310 --> 00:12:27.601
alluded to this before as well.

00:12:27.601 --> 00:12:30.180
Are there are tools that we
can use for container security

00:12:30.180 --> 00:12:33.387
so that we know, hey, this
came from developers, or hey,

00:12:33.387 --> 00:12:35.720
this is actually the thing
that I was meant to download,

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:36.450
and I downloaded it.

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:38.324
And it wasn't a problem
in somewhere between.

00:12:38.324 --> 00:12:39.350
And stuff like that.

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:39.840
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Yeah.

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:42.060
So typically when you're
setting up your environment,

00:12:42.060 --> 00:12:44.130
you're going to have a
container registry of some kind.

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:46.140
You're going to have somewhere
you put all your images.

00:12:46.140 --> 00:12:48.598
And Google offers what is called
Google Container Registry.

00:12:48.598 --> 00:12:49.860
We're great at naming.

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:51.256
And it integrates with GKE.

00:12:51.256 --> 00:12:53.880
And the idea being you store all
the stuff that you want there.

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:57.210
You can check things like, did
this go through QA and testing,

00:12:57.210 --> 00:12:58.706
all of that before deploying.

00:12:58.706 --> 00:13:00.330
What we actually just
announced at Next

00:13:00.330 --> 00:13:02.290
is binary authorization.

00:13:02.290 --> 00:13:04.170
So binary authorization
is a way for you

00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:07.440
to enforce that the container
images that you deploy

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:11.042
are those that you authorize,
that came out of your pipeline.

00:13:11.042 --> 00:13:12.750
So the main use case
we're going for here

00:13:12.750 --> 00:13:14.850
is, I have a build pipeline.

00:13:14.850 --> 00:13:17.820
I'm using CircleCI,
[INAUDIBLE],, Jenkins,

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:18.930
whatever happens to be.

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:21.970
And I give that thing
a private sign-in key.

00:13:21.970 --> 00:13:24.540
It signs that it did in
fact build that image.

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:27.380
And then I have it an emission
controller that enforces,

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:30.090
did that signature exist on
the image before I deployed?

00:13:30.090 --> 00:13:32.870
And if the signature does not
exist, it will not be deployed.

00:13:32.870 --> 00:13:34.770
So that's called
binary authorization.

00:13:34.770 --> 00:13:37.330
Some other cases you can imagine
around that are things like,

00:13:37.330 --> 00:13:39.060
hey, I don't want to
deploy images that

00:13:39.060 --> 00:13:40.205
have known vulnerabilities.

00:13:40.205 --> 00:13:41.580
I only want to
deploy images that

00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:45.010
came out of my particular
repository, et cetera.

00:13:45.010 --> 00:13:48.030
So you can think about that
type of policy management

00:13:48.030 --> 00:13:48.840
in that space.

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:51.048
MELANIE WARRICK: You were
saying before about gVisor.

00:13:51.048 --> 00:13:53.640
Where would that play a role
in the way this is built out?

00:13:53.640 --> 00:13:55.223
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
Yeah, I think gVisor

00:13:55.223 --> 00:13:58.950
is a great tool for anytime
you have untrusted workloads

00:13:58.950 --> 00:14:01.330
that you want to try bin
pack closer together.

00:14:01.330 --> 00:14:03.729
And then if you also have
even trusted workloads

00:14:03.729 --> 00:14:06.270
that you think they are still
going to work fine, like to me,

00:14:06.270 --> 00:14:07.980
that seems like
a no regret move.

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:10.063
Why wouldn't you want to
do a little bit more work

00:14:10.063 --> 00:14:11.610
and get a lot more security?

00:14:11.610 --> 00:14:12.960
MELANIE WARRICK: Under the
three areas that you mentioned

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:15.540
in terms of security, one
question that comes to mind

00:14:15.540 --> 00:14:20.882
is, what's the hardest to be
able to secure for containers?

00:14:20.882 --> 00:14:23.340
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: I think we're
seeing people kind of shift

00:14:23.340 --> 00:14:25.140
from left to right,
in that the first one

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:27.050
being infrastructure, then
software supply chain, and then

00:14:27.050 --> 00:14:28.050
container runtime.

00:14:28.050 --> 00:14:30.195
A lot of developers are
still focused right now on,

00:14:30.195 --> 00:14:32.820
how do I make Kubernetes secure
and what are the best practices

00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:33.930
I follow there?

00:14:33.930 --> 00:14:35.970
And where we're sort of
failing as a community

00:14:35.970 --> 00:14:38.100
is that it's so
hard for a developer

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:39.650
to understand what
they need to do.

00:14:39.650 --> 00:14:40.470
What I mean by that is--

00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:42.480
I mentioned I started working
in container security about nine

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:42.930
months ago.

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:44.430
I thought it would take
me like a month or two

00:14:44.430 --> 00:14:45.660
to figure out what
was going on in space.

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:46.450
And I'd be like, yeah, yeah.

00:14:46.450 --> 00:14:48.491
And then I've got container
security figured out.

00:14:48.491 --> 00:14:49.269
It'll be fine.

00:14:49.269 --> 00:14:51.060
And it probably took
me four or five months

00:14:51.060 --> 00:14:53.760
to sort of get a good
handle on what was going on.

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:56.430
And I was like, oh, man,
I'm getting old and tired.

00:14:56.430 --> 00:14:58.870
And why is this
taking me so long?

00:14:58.870 --> 00:15:00.330
I'm not as good as I used to be.

00:15:00.330 --> 00:15:02.170
This is terrible.

00:15:02.170 --> 00:15:05.040
MELANIE WARRICK: No imposter
syndrome going on there at all.

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:06.280
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: That's
not common at Google at all.

00:15:06.280 --> 00:15:07.860
What are you talking about?

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:11.090
And then I went to KubeCon
in Copenhagen this past May.

00:15:11.090 --> 00:15:13.590
And at that point in time, I
had a pretty good understanding

00:15:13.590 --> 00:15:14.640
of what was going on.

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:17.560
And so many people came up
to me and said, how do I

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:18.632
set up a network policy?

00:15:18.632 --> 00:15:20.090
What is RBAC and
should I use that?

00:15:20.090 --> 00:15:21.800
And these are-- to me,
these are like the basics

00:15:21.800 --> 00:15:23.716
in container security
and Kubernetes security.

00:15:23.716 --> 00:15:24.696
And it kind of hit me.

00:15:24.696 --> 00:15:26.060
It was like, oh, my God.

00:15:26.060 --> 00:15:28.220
This is way too
complicated for anyone.

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:29.120
MARK MANDEL: Right.

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:30.050
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
And so the challenge

00:15:30.050 --> 00:15:32.060
I think we have right
now is not so much that--

00:15:32.060 --> 00:15:34.250
sure, there are areas that the
tooling doesn't even exist yet,

00:15:34.250 --> 00:15:36.541
or we haven't figured out
how to solve the problem yet,

00:15:36.541 --> 00:15:39.140
but the stuff that we
have built is pretty hard

00:15:39.140 --> 00:15:41.294
to use for an average
developer, or the defaults

00:15:41.294 --> 00:15:43.710
that are there aren't great
for the average developer yet.

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:45.860
So there's a lot being done in
Kubernetes and the open source

00:15:45.860 --> 00:15:46.554
to improve that.

00:15:46.554 --> 00:15:48.220
But if you're starting
off from scratch,

00:15:48.220 --> 00:15:49.928
you can google Hardening
Guide and you'll

00:15:49.928 --> 00:15:51.950
find lots of different
people's opinions

00:15:51.950 --> 00:15:53.155
as to what you should do.

00:15:53.155 --> 00:15:55.530
MARK MANDEL: So what are your
opinions on best practices?

00:15:55.530 --> 00:15:58.113
Both probably on the container
side and on the Kubernetes side

00:15:58.113 --> 00:15:58.613
as well.

00:15:58.613 --> 00:16:00.321
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: On
the Kubernetes side,

00:16:00.321 --> 00:16:02.510
there's a piece of
documentation on the GKE site,

00:16:02.510 --> 00:16:06.560
g.co/GKE/hardening, which
is our Hardening Guide.

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:09.640
And it goes through what is
hardened for you already in GKE

00:16:09.640 --> 00:16:11.150
and what else you need to do.

00:16:11.150 --> 00:16:13.994
Some really basics are, hey,
you should probably use RBAC.

00:16:13.994 --> 00:16:15.410
If you're using
Kubernetes Engine,

00:16:15.410 --> 00:16:17.160
use IAM at the project
level, and then use

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:19.251
RBAC at the cluster
and namespace level.

00:16:19.251 --> 00:16:21.500
Set up a network policy so
pods can talk to each other

00:16:21.500 --> 00:16:23.180
by default within the cluster.

00:16:23.180 --> 00:16:24.680
That's great if
you're developing.

00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:27.680
That's terrible if you're
having supersensitive workload

00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:29.919
running next to each
other in the same cluster.

00:16:29.919 --> 00:16:31.460
And then from there,
just some things

00:16:31.460 --> 00:16:33.954
like setting a seccomp
profile is a great choice.

00:16:33.954 --> 00:16:35.870
You can enable that using
pod security policy.

00:16:35.870 --> 00:16:37.850
It provides a pretty
strong security default

00:16:37.850 --> 00:16:38.780
for your containers.

00:16:38.780 --> 00:16:41.071
MARK MANDEL: Can you explain
what a seccomp profile is?

00:16:41.071 --> 00:16:42.530
Because I have vaguely an idea.

00:16:42.530 --> 00:16:44.238
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
Yeah, a seccomp profile

00:16:44.238 --> 00:16:47.030
is a-- seccomp is
secure computing mode.

00:16:47.030 --> 00:16:50.630
And it puts your container sort
of like in a one-way secure

00:16:50.630 --> 00:16:53.330
state-- and I'm doing
air quotes here--

00:16:53.330 --> 00:16:55.940
where it can run a
certain set of syscalls.

00:16:55.940 --> 00:16:58.380
And if it tries to run things
like secreturn and exit,

00:16:58.380 --> 00:17:00.440
if it tries to run any
other set of syscalls,

00:17:00.440 --> 00:17:01.890
then it will be terminated.

00:17:01.890 --> 00:17:04.160
So it's preventing
your container

00:17:04.160 --> 00:17:06.619
from potentially running
something that would be unsafe.

00:17:06.619 --> 00:17:07.760
There's a pretty good
default out there.

00:17:07.760 --> 00:17:09.440
It's the Docker seccomp profile.

00:17:09.440 --> 00:17:15.505
It blocks about 150 uncommon
or barely used syscalls.

00:17:15.505 --> 00:17:17.630
And that should probably
work for your application,

00:17:17.630 --> 00:17:20.270
but test it out, obviously,
before you deploy it at scale.

00:17:20.270 --> 00:17:21.760
MELANIE WARRICK: And I know
you've mentioned RBAC before,

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:23.180
but I don't know what RBAC is.

00:17:23.180 --> 00:17:24.710
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Oh, sorry.

00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:27.660
RBAC is Role-Based
Access Control.

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:29.960
It's a way of thinking
about policy management.

00:17:29.960 --> 00:17:32.570
It's a generic term for thinking
about policy management.

00:17:32.570 --> 00:17:36.680
In Kubernetes specifically,
it's how you bind the access

00:17:36.680 --> 00:17:40.010
to certain resources, like pods
or deployments or namespaces

00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:41.400
to individual users.

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:44.709
And so you create a role
that has a set of permissions

00:17:44.709 --> 00:17:45.750
on a particular resource.

00:17:45.750 --> 00:17:48.290
For example, I
can read this pod,

00:17:48.290 --> 00:17:51.791
and then you would bind that
between the role to a user.

00:17:51.791 --> 00:17:53.540
The idea then you can
change the bindings.

00:17:53.540 --> 00:17:55.247
So if somebody quits
their job tomorrow,

00:17:55.247 --> 00:17:57.080
rather than removing
individual permissions,

00:17:57.080 --> 00:17:58.700
you just remove the
role, and then they've

00:17:58.700 --> 00:17:59.590
lost those permissions.

00:17:59.590 --> 00:18:00.230
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

00:18:00.230 --> 00:18:02.438
Do you have any tools that
you'd recommend for people

00:18:02.438 --> 00:18:04.147
to use to automate
security in the space?

00:18:04.147 --> 00:18:05.771
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: I
see a lot of people

00:18:05.771 --> 00:18:07.590
using some sort of
templating language.

00:18:07.590 --> 00:18:09.710
So Terraform is pretty
popular, or Helm.

00:18:09.710 --> 00:18:12.349
That's typically the
choice people go with.

00:18:12.349 --> 00:18:14.390
MARK MANDEL: Is there any
sort of automated tools

00:18:14.390 --> 00:18:17.060
for just making sure there
are no vulnerabilities

00:18:17.060 --> 00:18:18.350
in my containers?

00:18:18.350 --> 00:18:19.340
Is that a thing?

00:18:19.340 --> 00:18:20.120
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
Yeah, so you're

00:18:20.120 --> 00:18:21.740
probably going to look at
vulnerabilities in your images,

00:18:21.740 --> 00:18:24.590
because what's pretty cool
about containers versus VMs

00:18:24.590 --> 00:18:27.410
and things is that rather than
changing single lines of code,

00:18:27.410 --> 00:18:29.170
you actually change
that line of code,

00:18:29.170 --> 00:18:31.100
but then you redeploy
the whole container.

00:18:31.100 --> 00:18:32.120
So you have this
enforcement point,

00:18:32.120 --> 00:18:34.661
which is your deployment point,
where you see everything that

00:18:34.661 --> 00:18:36.110
ends up in your infrastructure.

00:18:36.110 --> 00:18:39.049
So actually what that means is
you get this very powerful way

00:18:39.049 --> 00:18:41.090
of patching your infra,
which is that if you just

00:18:41.090 --> 00:18:43.940
patch your images
on a regular basis,

00:18:43.940 --> 00:18:46.954
you can kind of passively
deploy the patch.

00:18:46.954 --> 00:18:48.870
As containers die, it'll
grab the latest image

00:18:48.870 --> 00:18:51.140
and get redeployed, which
is amazing, which is,

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:52.640
again, think back
at like how Google

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:54.140
does live migration with Borg.

00:18:54.140 --> 00:18:56.140
And like this is why we
can do things like that.

00:18:56.140 --> 00:18:57.110
It's genius.

00:18:57.110 --> 00:18:59.027
So specifically, how do
you patch your images?

00:18:59.027 --> 00:19:01.068
And how do you know what
the vulnerabilities are?

00:19:01.068 --> 00:19:03.170
Google just announced
Google Container Registry

00:19:03.170 --> 00:19:04.580
vulnerability scanning.

00:19:04.580 --> 00:19:07.130
And that looks at the packages
in your images and the images

00:19:07.130 --> 00:19:09.380
themselves-- maybe that's a
better way of putting it--

00:19:09.380 --> 00:19:11.170
that you have stored in GCR.

00:19:11.170 --> 00:19:13.880
We'll scan those automatically
for known vulnerabilities

00:19:13.880 --> 00:19:16.130
from the CVE database.

00:19:16.130 --> 00:19:18.440
And it'll also rescan
when new vulnerabilities

00:19:18.440 --> 00:19:19.400
are found or announced.

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:21.650
And then you can
patch those as needed

00:19:21.650 --> 00:19:23.990
and deploy them to
your environment.

00:19:23.990 --> 00:19:26.240
MARK MANDEL: Out of
curiosity, how does that work?

00:19:26.240 --> 00:19:28.790
Because you've got this
artifact that's an image,

00:19:28.790 --> 00:19:31.379
and then how does it
know how to get inside?

00:19:31.379 --> 00:19:31.920
I don't know.

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:33.374
That seems crazy,
but interesting.

00:19:33.374 --> 00:19:35.540
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: You
decompose the different layers

00:19:35.540 --> 00:19:36.050
of the image.

00:19:36.050 --> 00:19:37.670
So you get all the packages
that are inside them.

00:19:37.670 --> 00:19:39.080
And you take a hash
of the packages.

00:19:39.080 --> 00:19:40.850
And you compare that
against whether those

00:19:40.850 --> 00:19:43.021
are known to have that
vulnerability or not.

00:19:43.021 --> 00:19:43.520
Yeah.

00:19:43.520 --> 00:19:44.810
So that's not scanning.

00:19:44.810 --> 00:19:46.370
Notably, that's not
scanning at the code level.

00:19:46.370 --> 00:19:47.911
That's not scanning
things like, hey,

00:19:47.911 --> 00:19:49.410
did you leave the keys in code?

00:19:49.410 --> 00:19:50.868
Or did you have a
misconfiguration?

00:19:50.868 --> 00:19:51.740
Et cetera.

00:19:51.740 --> 00:19:54.160
There's a ton of third-party
solutions in that space that

00:19:54.160 --> 00:19:56.201
do provide that functionality
if that's something

00:19:56.201 --> 00:19:57.025
you want to do.

00:19:57.025 --> 00:19:59.150
MELANIE WARRICK: So what's
a software supply chain?

00:19:59.150 --> 00:19:59.670
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Oh, yeah.

00:19:59.670 --> 00:20:01.610
The software supply chain
is that middle piece

00:20:01.610 --> 00:20:03.560
that we were talking
about earlier-- so how

00:20:03.560 --> 00:20:07.280
we go from developing your code
to having an image to making

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:10.232
sure image is free of
vulnerabilities to checking

00:20:10.232 --> 00:20:11.690
that it meets all
your requirements

00:20:11.690 --> 00:20:14.180
when it's deployed, and
then to seeing that that

00:20:14.180 --> 00:20:15.729
doesn't change over time.

00:20:15.729 --> 00:20:18.020
So that's-- one big piece of
that is that vulnerability

00:20:18.020 --> 00:20:18.740
scanning piece.

00:20:18.740 --> 00:20:20.990
Another big piece of that
is the binary authorization.

00:20:20.990 --> 00:20:22.615
Basically, the
announcements that we've

00:20:22.615 --> 00:20:25.190
had around Next around
container security

00:20:25.190 --> 00:20:27.350
have been in that software
supply chain area.

00:20:27.350 --> 00:20:28.220
MELANIE WARRICK: I
know we've already

00:20:28.220 --> 00:20:30.303
talked a little bit about
resources, but are there

00:20:30.303 --> 00:20:33.800
any additional places people can
go if they wanted to know more,

00:20:33.800 --> 00:20:35.780
to get involved,
to do development

00:20:35.780 --> 00:20:37.732
in container security?

00:20:37.732 --> 00:20:38.690
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Sure.

00:20:38.690 --> 00:20:40.637
There's a couple
of links that we'll

00:20:40.637 --> 00:20:41.720
put somewhere on the site.

00:20:41.720 --> 00:20:45.296
They include cloud.google.com
/containers/security.

00:20:45.296 --> 00:20:47.670
It's kind of an overview of
how we think about containers

00:20:47.670 --> 00:20:49.340
at Google, including
a lot of the items

00:20:49.340 --> 00:20:50.549
that I've talked about today.

00:20:50.549 --> 00:20:51.923
And then I mentioned
specifically

00:20:51.923 --> 00:20:53.257
the Hardening Guide for GKE.

00:20:53.257 --> 00:20:55.340
It's another really great
spot if you're using GKE

00:20:55.340 --> 00:20:58.130
and want to have further
security than what we already

00:20:58.130 --> 00:20:59.990
provide you by default.

00:20:59.990 --> 00:21:02.457
MELANIE WARRICK: Well, as I
mentioned early in this podcast

00:21:02.457 --> 00:21:04.040
that we are going
to be releasing this

00:21:04.040 --> 00:21:06.170
the week before the
big brouhaha in Vegas

00:21:06.170 --> 00:21:09.110
with Black Hat and
DEF CON and BSides,

00:21:09.110 --> 00:21:11.330
can you tell the
audience a little bit

00:21:11.330 --> 00:21:13.560
about these conferences
that are coming up

00:21:13.560 --> 00:21:15.540
and what this whole
thing looks like?

00:21:15.540 --> 00:21:18.050
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: So this is
comically sometimes called

00:21:18.050 --> 00:21:19.510
like security summer camp.

00:21:19.510 --> 00:21:19.530
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:21:19.530 --> 00:21:20.236
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
Since all the security

00:21:20.236 --> 00:21:22.200
nerds get together in Vegas.

00:21:22.200 --> 00:21:23.477
And it's really hot.

00:21:23.477 --> 00:21:24.560
And we don't really sleep.

00:21:24.560 --> 00:21:26.476
MELANIE WARRICK: I know
August is not the best

00:21:26.476 --> 00:21:27.552
time necessarily to go.

00:21:27.552 --> 00:21:28.510
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Yeah.

00:21:28.510 --> 00:21:29.610
I was there last year.

00:21:29.610 --> 00:21:30.560
And I don't even know.

00:21:30.560 --> 00:21:31.976
I think I got sick,
because you're

00:21:31.976 --> 00:21:33.410
going outside from
like 40 degrees

00:21:33.410 --> 00:21:34.990
to like inside it's
like 50-- sorry.

00:21:34.990 --> 00:21:36.620
I speak Celsius-- 15 degrees.

00:21:36.620 --> 00:21:37.745
MARK MANDEL: I do the same.

00:21:37.745 --> 00:21:38.530
It's fine.

00:21:38.530 --> 00:21:40.380
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
And it's just insane.

00:21:40.380 --> 00:21:41.870
So DEF CON and all that stuff.

00:21:41.870 --> 00:21:43.610
So there's a Black
Hat conference, which

00:21:43.610 --> 00:21:45.200
is a big security conference.

00:21:45.200 --> 00:21:47.960
A lot of vendor shows, a lot
of research announcements,

00:21:47.960 --> 00:21:48.602
et cetera.

00:21:48.602 --> 00:21:50.060
The same time as
Black Hat, there's

00:21:50.060 --> 00:21:52.850
BSides Las Vegas, which
is an alternative security

00:21:52.850 --> 00:21:53.457
conference.

00:21:53.457 --> 00:21:55.040
And then at the end
of the week, there

00:21:55.040 --> 00:21:56.450
is DEF CON, which
is probably one

00:21:56.450 --> 00:21:58.200
of the more infamous
security conferences,

00:21:58.200 --> 00:22:01.419
a lot of live hacking,
zero days that

00:22:01.419 --> 00:22:02.960
are dropped as part
of presentations,

00:22:02.960 --> 00:22:03.752
that kind of stuff.

00:22:03.752 --> 00:22:05.668
MELANIE WARRICK: For
those who might not know,

00:22:05.668 --> 00:22:06.485
what are zero days?

00:22:06.485 --> 00:22:08.990
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: A zero
day is a newly-discovered

00:22:08.990 --> 00:22:10.795
vulnerability
which is unpatched.

00:22:10.795 --> 00:22:12.920
A great example would be
like Spectre and Meltdown.

00:22:12.920 --> 00:22:14.360
When those were
announced-- well,

00:22:14.360 --> 00:22:16.401
actually, maybe they're
not such a great example,

00:22:16.401 --> 00:22:18.780
because Google had patched
them when they were announced.

00:22:18.780 --> 00:22:19.280
Heartbleed.

00:22:19.280 --> 00:22:20.780
Heartbleed would
be a great example,

00:22:20.780 --> 00:22:23.840
where when it was announced,
it was not yet patched

00:22:23.840 --> 00:22:24.960
in the public domain.

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:27.837
And so it was immediately
exploitable by an attacker.

00:22:27.837 --> 00:22:29.670
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah,
we did a nice episode

00:22:29.670 --> 00:22:30.980
on Spectre and Meltdown.

00:22:30.980 --> 00:22:34.340
I talked a little bit about
why the security decisions are

00:22:34.340 --> 00:22:36.770
made in terms of when
to share information

00:22:36.770 --> 00:22:39.799
and trying to patch it
at the appropriate times.

00:22:39.799 --> 00:22:41.090
But that's great to understand.

00:22:41.090 --> 00:22:43.400
And so how many
years now has it been

00:22:43.400 --> 00:22:46.520
for you going to these events?

00:22:46.520 --> 00:22:48.610
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: It will
be my fourth DEF CON.

00:22:48.610 --> 00:22:50.902
MELANIE WARRICK: And what
are you most excited about?

00:22:50.902 --> 00:22:52.580
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: The
Crypto Privacy Village.

00:22:52.580 --> 00:22:54.871
I've been quite involved with
that village for a while.

00:22:54.871 --> 00:22:57.060
And I'm obviously
an encryption nerd.

00:22:57.060 --> 00:22:58.840
And that's the entire village.

00:22:58.840 --> 00:23:00.200
It's like getting a couple
of hundred people in a room

00:23:00.200 --> 00:23:01.540
together to talk
about encryption.

00:23:01.540 --> 00:23:03.170
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, I was going
to say, what's a village?

00:23:03.170 --> 00:23:04.586
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
So the main talk

00:23:04.586 --> 00:23:06.920
tracks at DEF CON, which are
in these giant convention

00:23:06.920 --> 00:23:10.872
halls, thousands of people
in the room, and even those,

00:23:10.872 --> 00:23:12.830
some of the best talks
you end up lining up for

00:23:12.830 --> 00:23:14.300
and still can't get into.

00:23:14.300 --> 00:23:17.720
So villages are
the smaller areas

00:23:17.720 --> 00:23:19.915
to have specific conversations
on particular topics.

00:23:19.915 --> 00:23:22.040
So for example, last year
they had a voting machine

00:23:22.040 --> 00:23:24.740
hacking village, where
some researchers brought in

00:23:24.740 --> 00:23:26.660
on the order of 20
or 30 voting machines

00:23:26.660 --> 00:23:28.714
and just basically
said, go for it.

00:23:28.714 --> 00:23:30.380
We'll write up
everything that you find,

00:23:30.380 --> 00:23:31.754
and send them to
some authorities

00:23:31.754 --> 00:23:34.215
and see if we can fix some of
the problems that are here,

00:23:34.215 --> 00:23:35.090
which is pretty cool.

00:23:35.090 --> 00:23:35.360
MELANIE WARRICK: That is cool.

00:23:35.360 --> 00:23:37.360
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: There's
a wall of sheep, which

00:23:37.360 --> 00:23:38.790
is all about packet capture.

00:23:38.790 --> 00:23:41.120
If you take your real
phone with you to DEF CON

00:23:41.120 --> 00:23:43.670
and have it kind of open
and connected to something

00:23:43.670 --> 00:23:45.740
that looks like it might
be a legitimate Wi-Fi,

00:23:45.740 --> 00:23:47.210
somebody is probably
just capturing everything

00:23:47.210 --> 00:23:48.080
that you're sending
them and then

00:23:48.080 --> 00:23:50.260
displaying it on a giant screen
for everybody else to see.

00:23:50.260 --> 00:23:51.676
MELANIE WARRICK:
Oh, that's great.

00:23:51.676 --> 00:23:53.240
So this is the
hazing that happens.

00:23:53.240 --> 00:23:54.130
MARK MANDEL: What
sort of preparation

00:23:54.130 --> 00:23:55.850
do you take to go to DEF CON?

00:23:55.850 --> 00:23:58.802
Do you take your proper phone?

00:23:58.802 --> 00:23:59.760
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: I do.

00:23:59.760 --> 00:24:00.290
I probably shouldn't.

00:24:00.290 --> 00:24:02.456
MARK MANDEL: Well, you
shouldn't say it on a podcast

00:24:02.456 --> 00:24:03.300
that you do.

00:24:03.300 --> 00:24:05.300
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: I used
to take a burner phone.

00:24:05.300 --> 00:24:08.259
And I think I got a
little bit complacent.

00:24:08.259 --> 00:24:09.050
MARK MANDEL: Right.

00:24:09.050 --> 00:24:10.010
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: I
do take a Chromebook.

00:24:10.010 --> 00:24:11.870
Chromebooks are great for
this kind of scenario, where

00:24:11.870 --> 00:24:13.286
it's like, I need
something that I

00:24:13.286 --> 00:24:15.140
can wipe if I have
any concern whatsoever

00:24:15.140 --> 00:24:17.270
with it being compromised.

00:24:17.270 --> 00:24:20.439
But nothing's ever happened
to me at Black Hat or DEF CON.

00:24:20.439 --> 00:24:20.810
MELANIE WARRICK: Knock on wood.

00:24:20.810 --> 00:24:22.089
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Yeah, yeah.

00:24:22.089 --> 00:24:23.380
MELANIE WARRICK: Not an invite.

00:24:23.380 --> 00:24:25.686
Well, any tips
for anybody who is

00:24:25.686 --> 00:24:27.560
going for the first time
or any of the people

00:24:27.560 --> 00:24:29.930
who maybe can't
go, but they want

00:24:29.930 --> 00:24:31.304
to learn more at a later date?

00:24:31.304 --> 00:24:32.720
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
Yeah, definitely

00:24:32.720 --> 00:24:34.469
go if it's something
you're interested in.

00:24:34.469 --> 00:24:36.290
It's a very
accessible conference.

00:24:36.290 --> 00:24:38.930
If you're having a bad time or
